-- 2026-10-26 (15): the flat satchel and a recipe book replace the pack's cloth screen (**NOT b1.7.3**; written WITHOUT a Rust toolchain: not compiled, not run, not seen on a device; +6 tests in `world/book.rs`, +7 in `satchel.rs`, `options_txt_round_trips` extended; run `cargo test --lib book satchel options` first). Request of the player: the 3D table was too dizzying to use all the time; a bag for the inventory and a book of every recipe beside it, the book's left page the categories then the list, its right page the 2x2 grid. `Screen::click`, `SlotId`, `pack::layout` / `sort` and the vanilla panels (furnace, chests, the pack switch off) are untouched; the cloth, `Mat`, the camera glide and the tabs stay in `pack.rs` and run only with `packCloth:true`.
-    - Screen (`satchel.rs`): `Frame::new(sat, inv, gw, w, h)` is every rect in px (fractions of the height, text unit `craft::panel(0, w, h).2`), `Frame::hit` says what a finger is on (`Pick`), `draw` / `draw_pin` paint it; `lib.rs` only opens it (`open_satchel`: the inventory button, or a workbench with the book already open), routes the finger (`on_sat_tap`, `sat_pick`, a drag on the list scrolls, a press on the result held `HOLD_SECS` crafts all) and closes it (`close_screen` puts the grid and the held stack back in the bag first). `TouchUi::pack_open` is set while it is up (every press a tap, the four HUD buttons hidden), buttons: Book, Sort, Shift, One above the bag, X at the corner. The bag rises in 0.25 s, the book opens in 0.3 s (both pages grow from the spine), a category slides in in 0.2 s. The world behind is dimmed.
-    - One layout (a first version had the cloth's four zones when closed; they sat mostly empty and the jump to the grid read badly): the bag is 9 x 3 + the belt + the armor row in 45% of the width (cells at least 8% of the height, checked by a test). Closed it is centred, the book button only slides it left (`opening_the_book_only_slides_the_bag`); every slot keeps its `SlotId` and always has its cell, so a held stack is put down by tapping a slot. Look, all drawn with quads (no texture): leather with a little grain, a seam of stitches and four rivets, a darker pocket band with a lit lip behind each row, a strap and buckle on the belt, and a slot rim tinted by `pack::category` (tools blue, food green, materials brown, other grey; the selected belt slot stays yellow). `pack::layout` / `ZONES` are no longer read by the satchel (the cloth still uses them).
